WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.959
Star Trek Beyond is a fun, fast-paced space
romp.

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:08.910
But just like any great action movie these
days, it still includes more than a few head-scratchers.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.790
Here's a look at some dumb things in Star
Trek Beyond everyone just ignored.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.249
Random chance

00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:19.990
Creepy bad guy Krall is looking for an alien
artifact that was chucked into space hundreds

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.020
of years ago.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.250
So how convenient is it that Kirk happens
to randomly end up with it after a totally

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.290
unrelated diplomatic mission?

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:31.680
It's also handy for Krall that Kirk kept it
on board his ship instead of sending it somewhere

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:32.680
safe.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.680
“Log that.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.410
Put it in the vault Spock.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.510
Thank you.”

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.670
Oh, and the fact that he brings it to the
closest possible outpost to Krall's base is

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.340
a pretty lucky break as well!

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.640
Y'know, considering the endless vastness of
space, and all.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.670
Dangling plot thread

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.090
When everyone takes the escape pods and heads
for Krall's planet, Scotty drops out late

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.269
and navigates his pod down to the surface.

00:00:55.269 --> 00:01:00.229
When he gets there, he realizes he's landed
it on the edge of a very big cliff, and finds

00:01:00.229 --> 00:01:05.280
himself in a classic Fast and Furious-style
action scene where he has to run up the falling

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.670
pod and grab hold of a tiny ledge hanging
off the cliff.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.240
Then we cut away, and the next time we see
Scotty, he's safely wandering around the planet

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.690
— at the bottom of the cliff?

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.600
So how did he get off the cliff?

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.070
Scotty is known for his big brain, but not
for his epic mountain climbing skills.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.689
Unless he jury-rigged a jetpack, we'd expect
the scene to end with him just falling to

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:28.170
his death.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.170
Those darn drones

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:33.820
So, Krall's whole thing to get this artifact
is to create a deadly bio-weapon.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.640
But since Krall already has a seemingly unstoppable
drone army capable of taking down Starfleet's

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.180
most advanced ships, why not just attack with
that?

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.180
Style points?

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.479
Plus, we find out those drones are based on
some ancient mining tech left on the planet.

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:54.120
So how did Krall turn a bunch of mining robots
into the universe's most dangerous fleet?

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.329
Does that mean some jerky supervillain will
just be able to weaponize all the drones we

00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:00.250
have flying around here on Earth?

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:06.369
“This amazing innovation then lowers itself
slowly to the ground, drops off the package,

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.060
and flies straight back up to altitude.”

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:14.160
Did we just figure out the real reason that
Amazon's Jeff Bezos made a cameo in the movie?

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.180
“Cheers.”

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.370
Total re-Krall

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.690
Jaylah has conveniently been living in the
U.S.S.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:26.739
Franklin for years, and has used a fancy bit
of cloaking tech to keep the ship hidden from

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:28.190
Krall and his minions.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:33.000
But once Krall is revealed to be the long-lost
captain of the Franklin, it begs the question:

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.770
How did he not already know where his ship
landed?

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:37.850
Space amnesia?

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.830
A long night of drinking space beers?

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.349
Yeah, it was probably the space beers.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:43.289
That's usually it.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.400
Beastie bikes

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.220
“Let’s make some noise.”

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:57.520
Okay, so no one's going to dispute that this
was probably one of the most fun scenes in

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:58.530
the movie.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.569
And yes, we love the Beasties Boys too!

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:05.510
But even though it's been a running gag since
2009's Star Trek reboot, the fact that the

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.250
Beastie Boys are still supposedly relevant
three centuries from now is actually pretty

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.250
silly.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.250
Don't agree?

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.830
Here's a quick quiz: name a pop star from
the 1760s.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.950
While you're googling that, we also have to
ask about the motorcycle that plays a pivotal

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.530
role in the finale.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.200
Why is there an ancient motorcycle on the
U.S.S.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.220
Franklin, and how is it still working after
all this time?

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.189
Between the bike and the Beastie Boys, it
seems like director Justin Lin really just

00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:36.090
wanted to make a cool '80s biker flick instead.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.530
Uhura's Spidey-sense

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.060
We get a peek at the U.S.S.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.680
Franklin's old crew footage midway through
the film, though Kirk and everyone else turns

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.480
away before Idris Elba appears in the frame.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.519
But Uhura circles back to the footage late
in the film, gets a look at the long-lost

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:54.099
captain, and immediately deduces that the
captain is actually Krall!

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.019
“It’s him.”

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:03.409
But...how?

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.670
He doesn't look anything like that anymore.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.980
Does she have facial recognition software
along with a GPS tracker in that necklace

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.040
Spock gave her?

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.970
“Lt. Uhura wears a Vokaya amulet which I
presented to her as a token of my affection

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:15.970
and respect.”

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.380
“You gave your girlfriend radioactive jewelry.”

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.820
“The emission is harmless, doctor.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.060
But its unique signature makes it very easy
to identify.”

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.940
“You gave your girlfriend a tracking device.”

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.460
“That was not my intention.”

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:34.840
Or maybe all that Vulcan radiation gave her
superpowers that let her see through Hollywood

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.490
prosthetics.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.150
Terrible security

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.470
When Krall makes his move and attacks Yorktown,
pretty much the only people around to help

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.840
stop the baddie from unleashing his virus
are Kirk and the members of his team, along

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.520
with, of course, Greg Grunberg.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.550
But this is supposed to be a fully-manned,
top of the line space station.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.000
So why are there no actual security measures?

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.210
The only time we see anyone at all is when
the Yorktown mall cops run right past the

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:05.370
bad guy like a bunch of, well, mall cops.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.960
Good job!

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.590
The big questions

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:15.169
A big chunk of Krall's plan is built on the
fact that he's able to hack into Starfleet

00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:19.710
and figure out where the artifact is to stage
his attack on the Enterprise.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:24.870
So if he can contact Starfleet, why didn't
he ever just, like, call them for help?

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.080
Isn't he like a few hundred years old?

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.770
How does he even know how to pull any of this
off?

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.520
How is Krall able to hack into Starfleet at
all?

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.270
Is he…a member of Anonymous?

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:37.270
Scotty wrote the script

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.919
Now, it's important when considering some
of the goofier aspects of Star Trek Beyond

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:44.880
to remember that Simon Pegg co-wrote the script.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.500
That's probably why Scotty can suddenly scale
sheer cliffs, finds a hot, space-elf girlfriend

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:53.990
right out of The Hobbit, and then somehow
manages to get the U.S.S.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:57.919
Franklin running again in a matter of hours
more than a century after it crashed face

00:05:57.919 --> 00:05:59.460
first into a planet.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:04.500
Knowing that Scotty is basically a walking
deus ex machina also explains how he was able

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:09.099
to teleport 20 people at a time during the
film's epic escape sequence.

00:06:09.099 --> 00:06:13.910
Just a few minutes earlier, he didn't trust
it enough to beam out two people at a time,

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:17.220
which is why Bones nearly got nabbed by Krall's
thugs.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.260
“At least I won’t die alone.”

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.400
“Well that’s just typical.”

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.150
But when the plot needs it, suddenly he's
totally cool with just transporting entire

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:33.050
football teams with no problems.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:34.050
Sure.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.690
“How many people can the Franklin transport
at a time?”

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.979
“Uh, with a wee bit of modification, 20,
max, but I’m not sure how long it would

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:40.979
hold out.”

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:45.470
Oh, and then there's the part where they "jump-start"
the Franklin just by flinging it off a mountain,

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:49.430
despite the fact that we're told the Franklin
wasn't designed to even function in the planet's

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.759
atmosphere.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:54.949
Of course, it's no surprise that he struggled
writing the 13th Star Trek film:

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:58.699
“It’s a fact, sure as day follows night,
sure as eggs is eggs, sure as every odd-numbered

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:01.160
Star Trek movie is s---.”

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:02.160
Hmmm...

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.160
Thanks for watching!

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.660
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.950
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

